# Error remediation

Wyrd errors carry structured data. An agent must preserve that structure and act on it directly, not collapse it to prose.

## Error shape

Wyrd JSON API errors follow RFC 9457 Problem Details with Wyrd extensions.
Two kinds of endpoint differ: the four OAuth 2.0 form endpoints described in
[OAuth endpoint errors](#oauth-endpoint-errors), and the browser sign-in
endpoints (`GET /auth/authorize`, `GET /auth/callback`, and `/auth/device`),
which may answer with a redirect or an HTML page instead. A registration
spec-drift response looks like this:

```json
{
  "type": "https://wyrd.dev/problems/WYRD_REGISTRY_409_SPEC_DRIFT",
  "title": "Card spec drift on re-apply",
  "status": 409,
  "code": "WYRD_REGISTRY_409_SPEC_DRIFT",
  "detail": "Card 'fraud-model@2.1.0' already exists with a different spec_hash; same-version cards are immutable.",
  "remediation": "Bump the card version, or re-apply the exact spec that was first registered at this version.",
  "details": {
    "card_ref": "fraud-model@2.1.0",
    "operation": "card.register"
  },
  "instance": "urn:wyrd:request:req_01hwz4k9m2e3f"
}
```

### Field reference

| Field | Description | Always present |
|---|---|---|
| `type` | URI identifying the error class | Yes |
| `title` | Human-readable summary of the error class, stable across instances | Yes |
| `status` | HTTP status code as an integer | Yes |
| `code` | Wyrd-specific machine-readable error code, stable string; use this for programmatic branching | Yes |
| `detail` | Instance-specific description of what went wrong | Yes |
| `remediation` | Actionable guidance for the caller or agent | Yes |
| `details` | Structured key/value map with operation-specific fields (card id, run id, policy name, denial reason, etc.) | When applicable |
| `instance` | Request correlation URN (`urn:wyrd:request:<id>`); the same id is also returned in the `wyrd-request-id` response header | When the server assigns a request id |

### OAuth endpoint errors

`POST /auth/token`, `POST /auth/platform/token`,
`POST /auth/device_authorization`, and `POST /auth/revoke` follow OAuth 2.0
instead. A refusal is the [RFC 6749
§5.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749#section-5.2) JSON body with
`error` and an optional `error_description`, sent with `400`, `401` for
`invalid_client`, `500` for `server_error`, or `503` for
`temporarily_unavailable`. It has no `code`, `remediation`, or `instance`, so
branch on `error`:

| `error` | Agent action |
| --- | --- |
| `invalid_request` | Fix the form parameters or the presented credential. Not a retry path. |
| `invalid_client` | Fix the client identification or client secret. Not a retry path. |
| `invalid_grant` | The code, refresh token, assertion, or credential is unusable. Obtain a new one; a person runs `wyrd auth login` again. |
| `unsupported_grant_type` | Check the exact `grant_type` value. |
| `authorization_pending`, `slow_down` | Keep polling the device code at the advertised interval (RFC 8628 §3.5). |
| `access_denied`, `expired_token` | Start a new device login. |
| `temporarily_unavailable` | Back off and retry. |
| `server_error` | Do not retry blindly; escalate. |

The Rust, Python, and TypeScript SDKs map these refusals onto the nearest
catalog code, so SDK callers keep branching on `code`.

## What agents must do

- Preserve all structured fields from the error response; do not flatten or summarize them.
- Use `code` for programmatic branching. `title` and `detail` are human-readable and may change in phrasing.
- Surface policy and audit denials as explicit decisions, not generic failures. The `code`, `detail`, and `details` together identify what policy was checked and what triggered it.
- When `remediation` is present, include it verbatim in any human-readable output or escalation.
- Keep retries bounded and tied to idempotent operations only. Check the `code` before retrying.
- Preserve the `instance` request URN (and the `wyrd-request-id` response header) in any escalation or support request.
- For `WYRD_CLIENT_400_TRANSPORT_FEATURE_DISABLED`, the
  `details.required_feature` names the Cargo feature that would
  re-enable the transport. The remediation is a build-time fix, not a
  retry path. Note: this error code is not yet emitted — it lands in
  PR4.0 when `WyrdClient::new()` is implemented.
- For `WYRD_CLIENT_400_CONFIG_INVALID`, the `details.field` and
  `details.reason` name the offending config field and the rule it
  failed. Fix the field on the config object and reconstruct the
  client. Not a retry path.
- For `WYRD_CLIENT_401_NO_CREDENTIALS`, the credential chain resolved no
  usable credential. The remediation is a config fix, not a retry path:
  set one of `WYRD_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `WYRD_WORKLOAD_TOKEN`+`WYRD_TENANT`, or
  `WYRD_API_KEY`; pass `ClientConfig::credential`; have a person run
  `wyrd auth login`; or add `[default].api_key` to
  `~/.config/wyrd/credentials.toml`.
- For `WYRD_CLIENT_503_TRANSPORT_DOWN`, the `details.transport` names
  the unavailable transport (DNS, TCP, TLS, or connect failure). Retry
  after the underlying network or server health recovers. Apply
  exponential backoff between retries.

## What agents must not do

- Do not re-wrap a structured error as an untyped string or generic failure message.
- Do not treat `status` as the full error identity. Two errors can share the same status code and require different handling.
- Do not ignore `remediation` when it is present.
- Do not infer error semantics from `detail`; it is instance-specific prose and is not stable across releases.

## Audit

Audit never refuses a request. A permission denial is reported by its
authorization code; a failure to record the decision is the server's concern
and never reaches the caller.

## Authorization errors

On Problem Details responses, and on SDK errors, use the Wyrd `code` value,
not HTTP status alone:

| Code | Agent action |
| --- | --- |
| `WYRD_AUTH_503_VERIFY_UNAVAILABLE` | Back off and retry the same operation. Do not re-authenticate; the credential was not rejected. |
| `WYRD_PERMISSION_500_ROLE_CORRUPT` | Do not retry. Surface the failure to a workspace admin; the stored role permission JSON must be fixed. |
| `WYRD_AUTH_401_INVALID_CARD_REF` | Re-issue the Service or Agent credential with `wyrd auth issue-key`. |
| `WYRD_AUTH_401_DELEGATION_DEPTH_EXCEEDED` | Do not retry. Contact the issuer; the token came from a non-conforming source. |
| `WYRD_AUTH_400_UNSUPPORTED_GRANT_TYPE` | Check the Wyrd version and exact `grant_type` value. |
| `WYRD_AUTH_404_PRINCIPAL_NOT_FOUND` | Verify the requested subject is a registered Service or Agent CardRef in the current tenant. User impersonation is not supported in this version. |
| `WYRD_AUTH_401_API_KEY_INVALID` | Do not retry with the same key. Rotate credentials via `wyrd auth issue-key` and update the deployment secret store. |
| `WYRD_PERMISSION_403_DENIED_RBAC` | Do not retry. The caller lacks the required RBAC permission. Request the required role from a workspace admin. |

## Retry behavior

Retries are only safe for idempotent operations. The `code` field is the signal for retryability:

- Safe to retry: read operations (card lookup, schema inspection, run query, policy list). These have no side effects.
- Unsafe to retry without idempotency key: card registration, run creation, policy writes. Re-sending these without a stable idempotency key may produce duplicate records.
- For `WYRD_REGISTRY_400_IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_INVALID`, send a new non-empty opaque `Idempotency-Key` header.
- For `WYRD_SPEC_400_DUPLICATE_SUBMISSION`, remove the repeated `(kind, space, name)` submission from the request.
- For `WYRD_REGISTRY_410_OPERATION_EXPIRED`, start a new registration with a fresh idempotency key.
- Not retryable: authorization errors. These require a corrective action before a retry will succeed.

If a Problem Details response does not include a `code`, treat the operation as non-retryable until the cause is identified. OAuth endpoint refusals are covered by their `error` value above.

---

Note: the public error catalog (one entry per `code` with retryability, context fields, and remediation patterns) is not yet emitted as a standalone file. Once the catalog generator lands, this page will link to the full per-code reference.
